# Tab-separated measurement data is parsed by column, not by line ending, and is
# read back on Linux runners as well as here. Pin it to LF so a checkout on a
# machine with core.autocrlf=true cannot put a stray CR in the last column.
#
# DELIBERATELY SCOPED TO *.tsv WHEN THIS WAS WRITTEN, and the reason given was that a
# repository-wide `* text=auto eol=lf` "renormalises every tracked text file on the next commit --
# a diff touching the whole repository", left as a separate decision. THAT DECISION WAS TAKEN ON
# 2026-10-10 AND THE WORRY WAS MEASURED AWAY: of 1 090 tracked files, 942 of the 947 text ones
# were ALREADY pure LF. Two were not, both from patch scripts that re-detected the line ending per
# write and disagreed with themselves, and three are testdata fixtures the `-text` rule below
# protects. So the repository-wide rule renormalised exactly those two files -- the same two the
# commit that added it was fixing -- and the feared whole-repository diff does not exist.
#
# SO THE LINE IS HERE FOR WHAT COMES NEXT, not for what is already stored. A file committed with
# CRLF under `core.autocrlf=true` -- the Windows default -- shows as modified in EVERY working
# tree forever, because the worktree copy normalises to LF and the blob does not match. No
# checkout can repair it; only a commit can. This stops the next tool from doing it again.
*.tsv text eol=lf
* text=auto eol=lf

# THE FIXTURES ARE QUOTED BYTES AND MUST NEVER BE TRANSFORMED. Everything under testdata/ is a
# fragment captured from a real server -- the first 512 bytes of a stored file, a server's own
# directory listing, a robots.txt -- and the tests assert the byte count and the sha256. Each
# .license sidecar states the exact count too, as the record of what was and was not reproduced.
#
# MEASURED 2026-09-29 ON THIS CHECKOUT, with core.autocrlf=true, the Windows default and what is
# set here: 100 of the 117 files under testdata/ are LONGER in the working tree than the blob git
# stores. html-behind-a-comment.jpg.head is 524 bytes where the fixture is 512 and its own sidecar
# says 512; every LF had become CRLF. heads-test.py and robots-fixtures-test.py both failed, on 16
# fixtures between them.
#
# THE REPOSITORY WAS NEVER WRONG -- `git show HEAD:<path>` returns the correct 512 bytes -- and CI
# on ubuntu-latest never saw it, because autocrlf is off there. So the defect is invisible from
# the one place that runs the tests automatically and certain on the machine the fixtures were
# made on. A fixture that changes between checkouts is not a fixture.
#
# `-text` AND NOT `text eol=lf`, WHICH IS THE OPPOSITE CHOICE FROM THE *.tsv LINE ABOVE and is
# deliberate. eol=lf still declares the file to be text, so git keeps the right to normalise it on
# the way in as well; these files may hold anything at all -- one is a truncated JPEG, another a
# GIF with its first byte removed -- and the correct instruction is that they are not text and
# nothing may touch them in either direction.
#
# "IN EITHER DIRECTION" IS NOT THEORETICAL -- TWO FIXTURES WERE ALREADY DAMAGED IN THE REPOSITORY,
# and it was the opposite direction from the one above. autocrlf converts CRLF to LF on the way IN
# as well, so the two heads whose real bytes CONTAIN CR were silently shortened when they were
# committed: geocities-under-pdf.head lost 2 bytes and ibm-marketing-under-pdf.head lost 5. Both
# were then 512-byte fixtures that were not 512 bytes, in a directory whose whole point is that
# the byte count is exact.
#
# NEITHER THE COMMIT NOR ANY CHECKOUT COMPLAINED. The failure surfaced only because heads-test.py
# records a sha256 per fixture and re-reads the file: the recorded digests were right and the
# committed files were wrong. Fixing the checkout direction alone would have left them broken and
# looking fixed, which is the worse outcome -- the tests would have gone green over bad data.
#
# THEY WERE RESTORED FROM THE MIRROR, not reconstructed: the first 512 bytes were re-read from
# ps-2.kev009.com/basil.holloway/ALL PDF/{cs4231a,sa380544}.pdf, and both digests then matched
# what the test had recorded all along. The originals were never lost -- only the copies were.
Python/PyMirror/testdata/** -text
